      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2022029158801453339",
      "url": "https://x.com/MCreekFutures/status/2022029158801453339",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/MCreekFutures/status/2022029158801453339",
      "text": "Thursday Wheat Report\n\nThursday, February 12, 2026\n\nThe wheat market is attempting a bullish pivot this Thursday as \"ice blast\" concerns in Russia provide a needed jolt to an otherwise heavy fundamental landscape. While Tuesday’s WASDE report confirmed a growing mountain of domestic supply, traders are shifting their focus to the \"what if\" of the 2026 global crop, injecting a weather-risk premium into a market that had been stuck in a supply-driven rut.\n\nFutures & Market News\n\nWheat futures are trading higher today, led by the Chicago (SRW) contract. The primary catalyst is reports of ice crusting over dormant wheat fields in Southern Russia, which can \"smother\" the crop and lead to significant winterkill. While the actual damage won't be known for weeks, it has triggered a round of short-covering by speculative funds.\n\nMarch 2026 (H): $5.40 1/4 (+3 1/4)\n\nMay 2026 (K): $5.48 0/4 (+2 3/4)\n\nJuly 2026 (N): $5.57 0/4 (+2 1/2)\n\nSeptember 2026 (U): $5.68 1/2 (+2 1/4)\n\nKey Driver: The \"Weather Trio\"—Russia, Europe, and the U.S.—are all following a similar, risky path: a mild start followed by extreme cold, and now a rapid warm-up that could melt protective snow cover and leave crops exposed to future freezes.\n\nUSDA Wheat News: February WASDE Highlights\n\nTuesday’s report remains the fundamental \"anchor\" for the market, painting a picture of ample supply:\n\nEnding Stocks: Raised to 931 million bushels (up from 926m in Jan), the highest since 2019/20.\n\nDomestic Use: Lowered slightly due to reduced flour milling demand, highlighting a sluggish domestic food-use trend.\n\nGlobal Perspective: World ending stocks were trimmed to 277.5 MMT, which remains a five-year high.\nThe Competition: Argentina’s production was raised to a record 27.8 MMT, ensuring that global export competition remains fierce through the spring.\n\nCommitments of Traders: Managed Money\nSpeculative funds are in the process of trimming their significant \"short\" positions as the weather headlines turn more volatile.\n\nNet Position: Managed money remains net short roughly 81,755 contracts in Chicago (SRW).\n\nTrend: Speculators covered over 12,000 short contracts last week; today's Russian weather news is expected to accelerate this trend.\n\nSentiment: The market is testing the $5.40 resistance level. If it breaks through, a more aggressive \"short squeeze\" could be triggered.\n\nExports Section: Competitive Struggles\n\nU.S. wheat is finding pockets of demand, but it remains a secondary choice on the world stage behind cheaper Black Sea origins.\n\nWeekly Inspections: Recent data showed 580,130 metric tons (approx. 21.3 million bushels) inspected.\n\nKey Buyers: The Philippines remains the top destination (179,957 MT), followed by Bangladesh and Mexico.\n\nTotal Sales: For the 2025/26 marketing year, total sales are trending 18% ahead of last year's pace, though this is barely enough to absorb the increased harvest.\n\nCrop & Weather Update\n\nDomestic (U.S. Plains): The February 10th Drought Monitor shows that 45% of winter wheat area is now impacted by drought (up 2% from last week). Warming temperatures in Kansas and Oklahoma are breaking crop dormancy early, making the plants more vulnerable to late-spring frosts.\n\nInternational: The Russian Ice Blast is the story of the day. Ice crusting over fields in key export regions is raising fears that Russia may fall short of its projected 90 MMT crop, which would significantly shift the global supply-and-demand balance.\n\nRegional Cash Bids (HRW & SRW Averages)\n\nBasis remains steady-to-soft as elevators wait for a more convincing rally before tightening bids.\n\nCentral Kansas (HRW)-0.63 H$4.64 3/4\nEastern Nebraska (HRW)-0.85 H$4.42 3/4\nSouthern Illinois (SRW)-0.34 H$5.06 1/4\nPacific Northwest (White)+0.38 H$5.78 1/4\n\nSummary & Strategies\nSummary: The market is transitioning from a \"WASDE hangover\" to a \"Weather Watch.\" While the 931-million-bushel ending stocks limit the immediate upside, the threat to the Russian crop is a credible catalyst for a relief rally.\n\nStrategies:\n\nProducers: For 2026 New Crop (July '26), look at the $5.60 – $5.75 range as a target for initial 15-20% hedging. These weather rallies are often short-lived in a high-stock environment.\n\nCommercials: Expect basis volatility in HRW regions if the U.S. Plains drought worsens during spring green-up.\nWatch Point: Monitor the \"thaw\" in Russia and the U.S. Plains over the next 72 hours. If snow cover disappears and temperatures remain volatile, the risk premium will likely expand.\n#Futures #Market #Wheat",
